# Pull Request Policy

## Draft default

Every new AI implementation delivered through GitHub MUST be published as one
GitHub Draft pull request. Set `draft: true`; do not add a `[Draft]` prefix to
the title. Routine authorization and the confidential-data boundary are
defined by `interactive-approval.mdc` and `github-safety.mdc`; this rule
defines the Draft PR contract.

## Required title and description

The title MUST be concise, outcome-focused, and in English. Apply a repository
pull-request title convention only when that convention is evidenced.

The description MUST be in English and contain the following information,
using these headings or equivalent headings from an applicable repository
template:

- **Problem / issue context:** the verified issue and the problem it defines.
- **Solution summary:** the implemented outcome supported by the plan and
  committed change.
- **Key changes and scope:** completed plan steps and exact committed paths;
  distinguish in-scope work from explicit non-goals.
- **Tests and validations:** every supplied check with its exact result and
  evidence; failed, skipped, and not-run checks must retain that status.
- **Known limitations:** documented deviations, warnings, unavailable inputs,
  unresolved questions, and explicit out-of-scope behavior.
- **Risks:** each evidenced implementation risk, severity, and mitigation;
  do not invent risks or claim that a mitigation was completed without proof.
- **Issue linkage:** exactly the verified issue, by default as
  `Fixes owner/repository#number` so GitHub closes it after a merge into the
  repository's default branch. Use `Refs` only when an explicit close-on-merge
  opt-out or an applicable repository convention selects neutral linkage;
  `Closes` and `Resolves` remain valid explicit closing-keyword choices.

Do not claim tests, validations, scope, behavior, limitations, risks, or issue
relationships that are absent from the supplied evidence. Do not infer a
second issue from prose, a branch name, a filename, or a commit message.

## Validation gate

Before creating a pull request, the implementation result MUST be validated.
Block pull-request creation when any of the following is true:

- `ValidationResult.status` is not `passed`.
- Any validation `blocker` is unresolved, or the scope gate is blocked,
  uncertain, or awaiting clarification.
- `readiness.draft_pr_preparation_allowed` is not `true`.
- A required check is failed, skipped, or not run.
- Acceptance or completion criteria are not all passed.
- Scope drift, unexpected changes, or documented deviations remain unresolved.
- The input `PullRequestDraft.status` is `blocked` or `partial`.
- The exact repository, issue, branches, committed scope, head SHA, or
  task-scoped draft-publication authorization cannot be verified.

Readiness is not authorization. Apply `interactive-approval.mdc` for the
routine publication gate and `github-safety.mdc` for the global safety floor.
Do not create a pull request from a warning, partial result, unresolved
blocker, or ambiguous handoff.

## Deterministic pre-publication gate

Immediately before `gh pr create`, the `create-draft-pr` Skill MUST write a
current local version-2 `PrePrCreateGate` containing the exact active
`BranchWorkspace`, passed `ValidationResult`, created `CommitProposal`,
verified non-force `BranchPush`, linked `PullRequestIssueLink`, and unpublished
`PullRequestDraft`. The host `pre-pr-create` Hook MUST fail closed unless the
gate, live HEAD, live remote branch, exact command, complete English
description, and unique issue link all match.

The Hook MUST only read the gate, command, body file, and live Git state. It
MUST never invent or rewrite a title, body, issue link, branch, SHA, or
authorization, and it MUST report concrete missing prerequisites when it
blocks. A missing, malformed, stale, identity-mismatched, partial, or
blocker-bearing gate prevents Draft PR creation.

## Existing pull requests and duplicate prevention

Check for an existing open pull request with the exact head-branch to
base-branch target before creating one, and repeat the check immediately before
the write:

- **No matching open pull request:** create exactly one Draft pull request
  with the approved title and description.
- **Exactly one matching open pull request:** never create a duplicate. Read
  and verify its repository, branches, head SHA, title, description, issue
  linkage, and Draft state. If any required value differs, return a partial
  result and do not edit it or mark it ready.
- **More than one matching open pull request:** block the workflow as
  ambiguous; do not select one and do not create another.

A closed or merged pull request does not justify creating a second open
pull request when the target is ambiguous. Never fabricate a pull-request
number, URL, or verification result.

## Ready-for-review and merge boundary

The Draft workflow MUST NOT request reviewers, mark a pull request
Ready-for-Review, or change pull-request metadata. Ready-for-Review is a
separate workflow owned by `/ready-pr` and `mark-pr-ready`. Rebase, merge,
and cleanup remain separate workflows under `interactive-approval.mdc`,
`github-safety.mdc`, and `merge-policy.mdc`.

## Ready-for-Review workflow

The explicitly invoked `/ready-pr` workflow MAY mark exactly one open Draft
pull request Ready-for-Review after independent authorization for that
repository, pull-request number, current head SHA, unique linked issue, and
optional reviewer set.

MUST:

- Keep unique issue linkage. Missing, mentioned-only, or ambiguous issue
  candidates block the transition.
- Write a current local version-1 `PrePrReadyGate` immediately before
  `gh pr ready`. The host `pre-pr-ready` Hook MUST fail closed unless the
  gate, live identity, Draft state, unique issue, command, and authorized
  reviewer set match.
- Treat an already non-Draft open pull request as `already_ready` after
  identity verification. Do not request additional reviewers.
- Request reviewers only from the authorized `reviewers.add` set after the
  ready transition. An empty set is valid. CODEOWNERS matches are optional
  suggestions and MUST NOT be treated as merge-required policy.
- Treat pending or failed CI as a warning, not a Ready-for-Review blocker.
  `MergeReadiness.status: ready` is not a prerequisite.

MUST NOT:

- Infer Ready-for-Review authorization from Draft publication, review
  publication, routine delivery, lifecycle, review-fix, feedback, or merge
  readiness.
- Convert a ready pull request back to Draft, edit title or body, add labels
  or assignees, merge, rebase, or publish a review as part of this workflow.
